          kind: "explain",
          title: "Is the reflex broken, or working against something?",
          body:
            "[[orthostatic-hypotension|Orthostatic hypotension]] is a finding, not a diagnosis. The first question is why the pressure falls.\n\nNon-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension: the [[baroreflex|baroreflex]] works, but something defeats it. Common causes are low blood volume (vomiting, diarrhea, bleeding, diuretics), drugs that dilate vessels or block alpha receptors, heart failure or a pump that cannot raise its output, and deconditioning after bed rest. Because the reflex is intact, the heart rate climbs steeply as the pressure falls.\n\nNeurogenic orthostatic hypotension: the sympathetic nerves fail to release norepinephrine onto blood vessels when the person stands. The vessels do not tighten, and the heart is not driven faster either, so the pressure falls with little rise in heart rate.\n\nA practical idea follows: compare the size of the heart rate rise with the size of the systolic fall. A small heart rate rise for a large fall in pressure points to a neurogenic cause; a large rise points to a non-neurogenic one. This is a guide, not a rule. Beta blockers, pacemakers, and sinus node disease also blunt the heart rate, and a person with autonomic failure can also be dehydrated.",
