            mechanism: "Standing pools blood below the heart. Baroreceptors sense the fall and the medulla increases sympathetic outflow: norepinephrine released from postganglionic nerve endings narrows arterioles and veins, and vagal withdrawal with sympathetic drive speeds the heart. In neurogenic failure this efferent limb is lost at some level, so neither vessels nor heart respond in proportion to the fall.",
            compare: "Look at the setting as well as the numbers. Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension tends to be chronic and comes with other autonomic symptoms: constipation, bladder problems, erectile dysfunction, reduced sweating, and dizziness after meals or in heat. Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension usually has an obvious trigger such as a new drug, an illness with fluid loss, or bleeding.",
