          title: "Brain or nerves: where the failure sits",
          body:
            "Once the cause looks neurogenic, ask where the autonomic pathway has failed.\n\nCentral causes are brain diseases, mainly the [[synucleinopathy|synucleinopathies]]:\n- [[multiple-system-atrophy|Multiple system atrophy]] (MSA): early, often severe autonomic failure (orthostatic hypotension, urinary retention or incontinence, erectile dysfunction) with [[parkinsonism|parkinsonism]] that responds poorly to levodopa, [[ataxia|cerebellar ataxia]], or both. Night-time inspiratory stridor, rapid progression, and early falls are typical. The lesion is in central autonomic neurons in the brainstem and spinal cord; the peripheral sympathetic nerves are largely preserved.\n- Parkinson disease: autonomic symptoms such as constipation are common and can come years before the movement disorder, and orthostatic hypotension becomes more frequent as the disease advances. Here the postganglionic sympathetic nerves to the heart and vessels also degenerate.\n- Dementia with Lewy bodies: autonomic failure, including orthostatic hypotension, can be prominent, alongside fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism.\n\nPeripheral causes damage the autonomic nerves or ganglia:\n- Diabetic autonomic neuropathy, the most common cause, usually alongside a length-dependent sensory neuropathy.\n- Amyloid neuropathy (light chain or hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis): a painful [[small-fiber-neuropathy|small-fiber neuropathy]] with prominent autonomic failure, weight loss, gut dysmotility, and often carpal tunnel syndrome.\n- [[autoimmune-autonomic-ganglionopathy|Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy]]: subacute, widespread autonomic failure, often after an infection, with antibodies against the ganglionic acetylcholine receptor. Some cases are paraneoplastic.\n- [[guillain-barre-syndrome|Guillain-Barre syndrome]]: acute [[dysautonomia|dysautonomia]] with swings in blood pressure and heart rhythm disturbances, which is one reason patients need close monitoring.",
