            simpler: "Autonomic failure can start in the brain (multiple system atrophy, Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies) or in the nerves that carry the signal out to the body (diabetes, amyloid, an antibody attack on the relay stations, Guillain-Barre syndrome).",
            mechanism: "Autonomic ganglia pass the signal from the preganglionic to the postganglionic neuron using acetylcholine acting on a nicotinic receptor that differs from the one at the neuromuscular junction. Antibodies against the ganglionic receptor block transmission in sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia at once, which is why autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy causes orthostatic hypotension, gut dysmotility, dry eyes and mouth, urinary retention, and poorly reactive pupils together, with strength and sensation spared.",
            compare: "Multiple system atrophy versus Parkinson disease: both can have constipation, erectile dysfunction, and dream enactment ([[rem-sleep-behavior-disorder|REM sleep behavior disorder]]). Severe autonomic failure in the first years, a poor or fading levodopa response, cerebellar signs, stridor, and rapid progression favor multiple system atrophy. A clear and lasting levodopa response and a markedly reduced sense of smell ([[anosmia|anosmia]]) favor Parkinson disease. Supportive MRI features are part of the 2022 criteria for clinically established MSA.",
            deeper: "[[pure-autonomic-failure|Pure autonomic failure]] is a synucleinopathy with autonomic failure alone and a postganglionic pattern. Some patients later develop Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, or multiple system atrophy, so the label is kept under review. In autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy, higher antibody levels tend to go with more severe failure; a positive result should prompt a search for an underlying cancer.",
