            mechanism: "Late phase 2 recovery and the phase 4 overshoot of the Valsalva maneuver both depend on sympathetic vasoconstriction, so they disappear in adrenergic failure. The QSART stimulus travels along one branch of a postganglionic sweat axon and back down another to release acetylcholine at nearby glands, so it tests only the postganglionic axon. The thermoregulatory sweat test needs every link from the hypothalamus to the gland.",
            compare: "Together the tests grade and localize failure. Diabetic neuropathy often affects cardiovagal and distal sudomotor function first. Multiple system atrophy gives severe adrenergic failure with sweat loss that is preganglionic in pattern. Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy affects all three systems at once.",
            deeper: "A composite score built from the adrenergic, cardiovagal, and sudomotor results summarizes the severity and spread of autonomic failure. Skin biopsy for intraepidermal nerve fiber density is a separate test of small sensory fibers. Results depend on preparation: caffeine, nicotine, a full bladder, and drugs with anticholinergic, sympathomimetic, or rate-slowing effects can all mislead.",
