            mechanism: "In POTS the heart rate rise is a compensation or overcompensation: when less blood returns to the heart on standing, or vessels in the legs fail to tighten, the heart speeds up to hold the pressure. In reflex syncope the efferent side of the reflex switches abruptly from sympathetic drive to vasodilation and vagal slowing, so pressure falls for seconds and cerebral blood flow fails.",
            compare: "Reflex syncope on a tilt table: pressure and heart rate are normal for several minutes, then fall together, reproducing the faint. POTS: the heart rate rises early and stays high, and the pressure holds. Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension: the pressure falls early and keeps falling while the heart rate barely moves. Apparent loss of consciousness with normal pressure and heart rate on tilt suggests functional (psychogenic) pseudosyncope.",
            deeper: "Initial orthostatic hypotension is defined as a fall of more than 40 mmHg systolic or 20 mmHg diastolic within 15 seconds of standing. It is common in healthy young people and older adults, needs continuous beat-to-beat measurement to capture, and explains lightheadedness in the first moments after standing up quickly.",
