          kind: "explain",
          title: "POTS describes a pattern, not a cause",
          body:
            "Meeting the heart rate criteria tells you what the circulation does on standing. It does not tell you why. POTS is a heterogeneous condition, and several contributors are recognized and often overlap: reduced blood volume, deconditioning (including after illness or bed rest), excess sympathetic activity, and a partial autonomic neuropathy affecting the legs. It often starts after an infection, surgery, or another physiological stressor, and it is more common in young women.\n\nSo the work after naming the pattern is to look for contributors and alternatives, not to stop. Ask about fluid and salt intake, recent illness or bed rest, weight loss, heavy menstrual bleeding, and drugs or stimulants. Check that the heart rate is normal at rest, which separates POTS from tachycardia that is present all the time.\n\nNonpharmacological measures come first:\n- Explanation that the symptoms are real and physiological, and avoidance of aggravating factors such as heat, large meals, alcohol, and prolonged standing still.\n- More fluid and salt, where appropriate: not for people with hypertension, heart failure, or kidney disease.\n- Compression garments covering the abdomen and legs, which reduce pooling.\n- [[counterpressure-maneuvers|Physical counterpressure maneuvers]] such as crossing the legs and tensing, clenching the buttocks, or squatting.\n- A gradual exercise program that starts with recumbent exercise (rowing, recumbent cycling, swimming) and moves upright as tolerance improves. Avoiding activity worsens deconditioning and symptoms.\nMedicines are added only for persistent symptoms, are used off-label, and the evidence for them is limited.\n\nReflex syncope follows the same logic: explanation and reassurance, recognizing and avoiding triggers, and counter-maneuvers or lying down at the first warning.",
