          title: "Treating neurogenic orthostatic hypotension",
          body:
            "The goal is to reduce symptoms and falls on standing and improve what the person can do, not to reach a target blood pressure. The 2017 consensus panel describes a stepwise approach.\n\n1. Remove or reduce drugs that lower standing pressure where possible.\n2. Nonpharmacological measures for everyone: rise in stages, avoid straining, heat, and large carbohydrate-rich meals or alcohol, use counter-maneuvers, wear abdominal compression, take adequate fluid and salt where appropriate, and sleep with the head of the bed raised.\n3. If standing symptoms still limit daily life, add a medicine: a volume expander (fludrocortisone) or a pressor (midodrine or droxidopa). Specialists sometimes use other agents. Short-acting pressors are given in the daytime, when the person is upright, not close to bedtime.\n\nEach step is a tradeoff with [[supine-hypertension|supine hypertension]]. Measure lying as well as standing pressure after every change. Raising the head of the bed and avoiding lying flat during the day lower supine pressure. When supine hypertension is severe, specialists may add a short-acting antihypertensive at bedtime. Falls and fainting are usually the more immediate danger, so treatment should not be stopped simply because the lying pressure is high.",
